You will refresh the page more times than you want to admit. Then the number appears, and it stops being a number you can change.

Everything after that is a different skill.

{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** NEET PG 2026 results are expected by 30 September on natboard.edu.in. Counselling then runs in four All India Quota rounds through MCC, covering 50% of seats, with your state authority running the other half. Your rank is fixed, but your choice list is not, and that list is where most seats are won or lost.
{% /callout %}

## What happens after the NEET PG result

The result is not the start of admissions. It is the start of a queue.

Once scores are out, two separate counselling processes run in parallel. The Medical Counselling Committee handles the All India Quota. Your state authority handles the state quota, with its own portal, its own dates, and its own rules.

{% statCallout
   number="50%"
   label="of PG seats go through All India Quota counselling run by MCC. Your state authority runs the other half, separately." /%}

In recent cycles, the All India Quota side has run in four rounds:

{% table %}
- **Round**
- **What it is**
- **What to know**
---
- **Round 1**
- First registration, choice filling, allotment
- Everyone starts here. A seat allotted is a seat you either take, or let go with consequences
---
- **Round 2**
- Fresh registration, fresh choices, remaining seats
- If you want to upgrade, you usually have to register again, even holding a Round 1 seat
---
- **Round 3**
- Further round on leftover seats
- The pool is smaller and less predictable
---
- **Stray vacancy**
- Final fill of whatever remains
- Fresh choice filling, and no upgrading once you accept
{% /table %}

Two things follow from that table, and most people miss both.

The first is that **holding a seat does not carry you forward automatically.** If you want a shot at something better in the next round, you generally have to re-register for it. People have sat out a round assuming their existing allotment rolled over, and found it did not.

The second is that **the rounds get worse, not better, as a planning environment.** Round 1 has the widest seat matrix and the most predictable behaviour. By stray vacancy you are choosing from a thin, strange list under time pressure.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**One caution worth repeating:** counselling rules are revised between cycles. Everything above describes how the process has recently worked, not a guarantee of 2026 procedure. The official information bulletin is the only binding version. Read it when it drops, even if you think you know the process.
{% /callout %}

## Why the choice list carries more weight than people expect

Here is the uncomfortable arithmetic. Your rank determines which seats are reachable. Your choice list determines which reachable seat you actually get.

Two candidates with nearly identical ranks routinely end up in very different places, and the difference is almost never luck. It is that one of them filled a list that reflected what they would genuinely accept, in a defensible order, and the other filled a list built out of hope and hurry.

You cannot revise your rank. You can revise your list until the moment it locks. That asymmetry is the whole argument for taking this window seriously.

## Five mistakes that quietly cost seats

### Filling too few choices

This is the big one. A short list feels decisive. It is not decisive, it is fragile. Every option you leave off is a seat you have pre-emptively refused, including ones you would have been relieved to get in November.

Adding a choice you rank low costs you nothing. Allotment works down your order, so a low-ranked option is only ever reached when everything above it is gone. There is no penalty for depth.

### Confusing what you want with what you would accept

Your list is not a wishlist. It is an instruction to a computer about what to do on your behalf in every scenario, including the ones you are trying not to think about.

A useful test for each entry: *if this were the last seat available to me, would I take it or sit the exam again?* If the answer is take it, it belongs on the list. If the answer is genuinely sit it again, leave it off, and mean it.

### Not understanding upgradation before you need to

Upgradation decides what happens to a seat you already hold when a better one becomes reachable. The mechanics vary by round and by cycle, and they interact with registration, with fees, and with whether you accepted or merely held the allotment.

Work this out while you are calm and the bulletin is in front of you. Do not work it out on the last evening of a round, which is when most people first read the rules properly.

### Treating All India and state quota as one decision

They are two processes, two portals, two sets of dates, and often two different strategies. State quota rules on domicile, reservation and bonds differ substantially between states.

Running both properly is more work than most people plan for. Track them separately, in writing, with their own deadlines.

### Making the reattempt decision at 2am in the last round

Whether to take a seat or try again is a real decision with a defensible answer either way. It is not a decision to make when exhausted, at the deadline, with a half-filled form open.

Make it in advance, in the abstract, while nothing is at stake. More on that below.

## How to build your list in three passes

Do not write the list in one sitting. Build it in layers, on three different days, so that each pass is made in a different mood.

1. **The long list.** Write down every college and branch combination that is plausibly within reach, with no ordering and no filtering. Be generous. This is the raw material, not the answer.
1. **The honest cut.** Go through the long list and remove only what you would genuinely refuse under any circumstances. Most people cut far too much here. If you hesitate, keep it.
1. **The order.** Now rank what survived. Branch first or city first is a real trade-off and there is no universal right answer, but you should be able to say out loud why your order is what it is.

Then leave it alone for a day and read it once more before locking. The version you write while tired is rarely the version you want submitted.

{% actionBox title="Do this in the window before registration opens" %}
- Get your documents scanned and in one folder now: degree and internship certificates, registration, identity proof, category and disability certificates where applicable
- Bookmark both portals, the MCC site and your state authority's site, and check them on a fixed schedule rather than continuously
- Read last cycle's information bulletin end to end so the new one takes ten minutes instead of an evening
- Write your long list this week, while there is no deadline pressure on it
- Work out the fee and bond position for the colleges you are seriously considering, before they become urgent
{% /actionBox %}

## Taking a seat, or going again

Nobody can make this call for you, and anyone selling a confident universal answer is selling something. But the decision gets clearer when you separate it into questions that have actual answers.

**What is the real cost of another year?** Not just the attempt. A year of income foregone, a year of seniority, the financial position of the people supporting you, and what another year of preparation would genuinely do to you.

**Is the branch the problem, or the college?** These get blurred together at the deadline and they are not the same constraint. One of them may be far more tolerable than the other.

**What would you tell a friend with your exact rank and your exact options?** People are consistently more sensible on behalf of other people. The advice you would give a friend is usually the advice you should take.

**What does the trend actually say?** Look at how cut-offs for your target seats have moved over recent cycles, not at one year in isolation. A single year is noise.

There is no version of this where you get certainty. There is a version where you make the decision deliberately instead of by default, and that is the one worth aiming for.

## What to verify when the official bulletin is out

Do not carry forward assumptions from last year, or from a senior, or from a WhatsApp group. When the 2026 bulletin is published, check these specifically:

- The exact round structure and the dates for each
- Registration, fee and refund rules, and the security deposit position for each round
- What happens if you are allotted a seat and do not take it, at each round
- Upgradation rules and whether re-registration is required
- Reporting deadlines and the document list, in full
- For your state: domicile, reservation and bond terms

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Sources that are worth trusting:** NBEMS at natboard.edu.in for the result, MCC at mcc.nic.in for All India Quota counselling, and your state authority's official portal. Aggregator sites are useful for a heads-up and unreliable for specifics. When they disagree with the official bulletin, the bulletin is right.
{% /callout %}

## The part nobody tells you

The gap between the result and the first round feels like dead time. It is the opposite. It is the last stretch of this process where you are making decisions calmly rather than reacting to a countdown.

The people who come out of counselling well are rarely the ones who got lucky in the last round. They are the ones who did the boring work early, while everyone else was still refreshing the result page.

